tion displayed. The user's name is SOL-04821, and their role is J. Employee.\n\n* **CoreID Admin Console**\n\t+ The top-left corner of the image displays the CoreID logo and the words \"Admin Console\" in white text on a dark blue background.\n* **User Profile Information**\n\t+ The main section of the image shows a white box with a light gray border, containing the user's profile information.\n\t+ The user's name is displayed in large black text at the top of the box, followed by their role in smaller text below.\n\t+ The user's ID number, SOL-04821, is displayed in a smaller text box below their name.\n* **Locked Button**\n\t+ A red button with a white lock icon is located in the bottom-right corner of the box, indicating that the user's account is locked.\n* **Settings Menu**\n\t+ A dark blue menu bar runs along the left side of the image, with the words \"Settings\" displayed in white text at the bottom.\n\nThe image suggests that the user's account is currently locked, and the support agent may need to investigate further to determine the cause of the lockout. The presence of the locked button and the user's profile information provides some context for the issue, but additional information may be required to fully diagnose and resolve the problem.\n\nStep 3: Caller identity verified\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Identity Verification panel\nAsk the caller for at least 2 of: employee ID, manager's name, department, or last 4 digits of the phone on file. ✅ Do not send a one-time code to the user — your own admin session already satisfies the authentication requirement; this checklist is what verifies the caller is who they claim to be. If fewer than 2 items match, stop and escalate to your team lead rather than proceeding.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 3 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a CoreID Admin Console page, with a white background and a dark blue sidebar on the left. The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\" and has a list of options below it.\n\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n    *   There are two checkboxes: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n    *   There is a dropdown menu with the option \"Department matches record\".\n    *   There is a checkbox for \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n    *   At the bottom of the page, there is a message that says \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\n**Summary:**\n\nThe image shows a screenshot of a CoreID Admin Console page, with a list of options for verifying an employee's identity. The page has two checkboxes, a dropdown menu, and a checkbox for last 4 digits of phone on file. The page also has a message at the bottom indicating that 2 out of 4 requirements have been met.\n\n**Detailed Description:**\n\n*   The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n*   There are two checkboxes: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n*   There is a dropdown menu with the option \"Department matches record\".\n*   There is a checkbox for \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n*   At the bottom of the page, there is a message that says \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n\n**Extracted Text:**\n\n*   \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\"\n*   \"Employee ID matches ticket\"\n*   \"Manager name matches ticket\"\n*   \"Department matches record\"\n*   \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\"\n*   \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\"\n\n**Errors and Warnings:**\n\n*   None visible\n\n**Likely Issue Category and Severity:**\n\n*   Category: Unknown\n*   Severity: Unknown\n\n**Observed Facts vs Inference:**\n\n*   **Observed Facts:**\n    *   The page is titled \"J. Employee - Identity Verification\".\n    *   There are two checkboxes: \"Employee ID matches ticket\" and \"Manager name matches ticket\".\n    *   There is a dropdown menu with the option \"Department matches record\".\n    *   There is a checkbox for \"Last 4 digits of phone on file\".\n    *   At the bottom of the page, there is a message that says \"2 of 4 confirmed - minimum required to proceed\".\n*   **Inference:**\n    *   The page is likely used for verifying an employee's identity.\n    *   The checkboxes and dropdown menu are likely used to collect information about the employee.\n    *   The message at the bottom of the page indicates that the verification process is not yet complete.\n\n**Confidence Score and Notes:**\n\n*   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n*   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n*   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear and well-lit, and the text is easy to read. There are no missing or unclear elements in the image.\n\nStep 4: Temporary password issued\nNavigation: CoreID Admin Console → user profile → Reset Password\nSelect Generate Temporary Password. Leave Force change at next sign-in checked — this is the default and it must not be disabled.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 4 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.95 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of a password reset page for an employee named J. Employee, with a temporary password provided and a button to generate a new one.\n\n*   **Reset Password - J. Employee**\n    *   The title of the page is \"Reset Password - J. Employee\".\n    *   Below the title, there is a temporary password: \"Tq7#mVr2lklp9x\".\n    *   A checkbox is available to force a change at the next sign-in.\n    *   A blue button labeled \"Generate Temporary Password\" is present.\n*   **User Interface**\n    *   The page has a white background with black text.\n    *   The left side of the page features a dark blue sidebar with the following options:\n        *   CoreID\n        *   Admin Console\n        *   Dashboard\n        *   Tickets\n        *   Users\n        *   Reports\n        *   Settings\n*   **Error Messages**\n    *   There are no error messages visible on the page.\n*   **Likely Issue Category and Severity**\n    *   The likely issue category is \"Account/authentication\".\n    *   The severity is \"Medium\" because the user needs to reset their password, but the process is straightforward and does not indicate any critical errors.\n*   **Observed Facts vs Inference**\n    *   **Observed Facts:**\n        *   The page is for resetting a password.\n        *   A temporary password is provided.\n        *   A button to generate a new password is available.\n    *   **Inference:**\n        *   The user may have forgotten their password or is requesting a password reset.\n        *   The temporary password is likely to be used until the user creates a new one.\n*   **Confidence Score and Notes**\n    *   CONFIDENCE SCORE: 0.95\n    *   CONFIDENCE LEVEL: High\n    *   CONFIDENCE REASON: The image is clear, and the text is legible, making it easy to understand the context and purpose of the page.\n\nStep 5: Temporary password relayed securely\nNavigation: Phone / voice channel — not logged in CoreID\nRead the temporary password to the caller verbally over the verified phone line. Never send it by email, chat, or ticket comment in plain text. See [CISA's guidance on requiring strong passwords](https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords) for the reasoning behind credential-handling policy.\nStep 6: Ticket resolved with audit note\nNavigation: Ticketing system → ticket → Resolution notes\nLog what was done (identity checks matched, temporary password issued, force-change enabled) and set the ticket to Resolved. This note is what satisfies the audit trail requirement — an admin-mediated reset with no note is a compliance gap.\n\n### Visual Inspection Report (Image 5 (inline, following the text above)) [Confidence: 0.90 / High]:\nThe image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n\n*   **Ticket Information**\n    *   Ticket Number: SOL-84213\n    *   Status: Resolved\n    *   Resolution Notes: Identity verified (2 of 4 checks). Temporary password issued via CoreID. Force-change-at-next-sign-in enabled. No OTP sent - admin-mediated reset per policy.\n*   **CoreID Admin Console**\n    *   The CoreID Admin Console is open, displaying the ticket resolution page.\n    *   The console has a dark blue background with white text.\n    *   The page has a light gray background with black text.\n*   **Navigation Menu**\n    *   The navigation menu is located on the left side of the page.\n    *   It includes options such as \"Admin Console\", \"Dashboard\", \"Tickets\", \"Users\", \"Reports\", and \"Settings\".\n*   **Ticket Resolution Page**\n    *   The ticket resolution page is displayed in the center of the page.\n    *   It shows the ticket number, status, and resolution notes.\n    *   The page has a light gray background with black text.\n\nIn summary, the image shows a screenshot of the CoreID Admin Console, with a ticket resolution page open. The page displays the ticket number, status, and resolution notes, and provides information about the identity verification process and temporary password issuance. The navigation menu is located on the left side of the page, and the ticket resolution page is displayed in the center.\n\nOutputs\nUser account unlocked with a valid temporary password\nForce-change-at-next-sign-in flag enabled\nTicket resolved with an audit note logged\nFAQs\nQ: What if the caller can't confirm at least 2 of the 4 identity items?\nDo not issue a reset. Escalate to your team lead, or ask the requester to retry self-service once they regain access to a verified phone or email.\nQ: Why doesn't this flow send a verification code the way self-service does?\nThe self-service code exists to authenticate an anonymous claimant with no other proof of identity. Here, identity is established two ways at once: your own CoreID session is already MFA-authenticated and scoped to the Password Reset Admin role, and the caller checklist independently confirms the requester is who they claim to be. Adding a code on top of that verifies nothing new — it would just be an unnecessary step to a channel the caller may not have working anyway, which is usually why the ticket was escalated in the first place.\n\n## LINKED REFERENCE KNOWLEDGE (Scraped from embedded SOP URLs):\n### Reference Web Page [https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords]:\nURL: https://www.cisa.gov/secure-our-world/require-strong-passwords\nStatus Code: 200\n\n--- SCRAPED MARKDOWN CONTENT START ---\n[Skip to main content](#main)\n\n![U.S. flag](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/us_flag_small.png)\n\nAn official website of the United States government\n\nHere’s how you know\n\nHere’s how you know\n\n![Dot gov](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-dot-gov.svg)\n\n**Official websites use .gov**\n\n A **.gov** website belongs to an official government organization in the United States.\n\n![HTTPS](/profiles/cisad8_gov/themes/custom/gesso/dist/images/icon-https.svg)\n\n**Secure .gov websites use HTTPS**\n\n A **lock** (LockA locked padlock) or **https://** means you’ve safely connected to the .gov website. Share sensitive information only on official, secure websites.\n\n[Staying Secure at Events](/staying-secure-large-scale-events)[no-cost Cyber Services](/resources-tools/resources/free-cybersecurity-services-and-tools \"Free Cyber Services\")[secure your business](/secureyourbusiness)[Known Exploited Vulnerabilities Catalog](/kev)[Report A Cyber Issue](/report)\n\n[![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Security Defense Agency National Coordinator For Critical Infrastructure Security and Resilience](/sites/default/files/images/SVG/header_logo_tagline_update.svg)![Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency logo America’s Cyber Securit